"executive_summary": "Monolayer transition metal dichalcogenides (TMDCs) are attractive for optoelectronics because of their three-atom-thick crystals and direct band gaps, but their photoluminescence (PL) is weak. Existing chemical treatments (p-dopants, superacids, oleic acid, defect repair) work mainly for sulfur-based compounds and not universally. This paper introduces a universal postpreparation method: coating monolayer TMDCs with a simple hydrocarbon, paraffin, which has a large thermal expansion coefficient. During spin coating and subsequent drying, the paraffin induces spontaneous fluctuation and eventual desorption of the monolayer from the substrate, removing the substrate effect and dramatically enhancing PL. The method was applied to MoS 2 , WS 2 , MoSe 2 , and WSe 2 , achieving enhancement factors of 215-, 55-, 50-, and 9-fold from the as-exfoliated flakes, respectively. In extreme cases, the monolayer deformed into a bundled shape. PL and Raman analyses support the mechanism: decreased carrier concentration, strain, and exciton-dominated emission. The authors argue the approach is universal across sulfur- and selenium-based TMDCs and could enable transparent, flexible optoelectronic devices. This summary is grounded in the paper's abstract and introduction, which state the gap and the universal strategy.",
